data 1173
# The tally constitution

What tally is for and its lasting values. The owner sets it and changes it.

## What tally is for

tally keeps what the owner spends in a file the owner can read and edit by hand, and answers questions about it
quickly: what was spent, where, and when.

## Article 1 (plain-text). The ledger is the owner's file

The ledger is a plain text file in the owner's hands: tally reads it and never needs anything beside it to make sense
of it. A ledger written ten years ago still reads.

Violations: a cache, an index or a database the ledger cannot be understood without; a format a person cannot edit in
a text editor.

## Article 2 (exact). Numbers are right or refused

A report is either exactly right or refuses to be made, saying why. Nothing is skipped, rounded away or guessed in
silence.

Violations: a line skipped because it cannot be read; a sum that drifts; a month that shows the wrong total.

## Article 3 (small). Small and fast

tally stays small enough to read in an afternoon, and fast on the largest ledger the owner keeps.

Violations: a dependency for what a few lines do; a report that takes seconds on a ledger of a few years.

blob
mark :20
data 340
### decision amounts-are-floats
An amount is an `f64`, read and written with two decimals.
Why: The ledger's amounts have at most two decimals and its sums are shown rounded to two, so a float's error never
reached a report; integer cents were turned down as one more type for every command to convert (a judgment, with no
case behind it).

blob
mark :21
data 324
### decision dates-are-checked-text
A date is kept as the text the ledger holds, checked only to be `YYYY-MM-DD` in shape.
Why: Every report needs the date only to group and sort by it, which the text does as it is; a calendar check (a 31st
of February) was turned down as a dependency for a mistake the owner has not made.

blob
mark :22
data 583
### decision a-month-is-a-text-key
A month of the monthly report is keyed by the first characters of the entry's date as text, `YYYY-MM`, not by a parsed
date.
Why: Dates are kept as text and checked only for their shape (decision in `entry`), so a key cut from the text sorts
in time order and needs no date type.

### decision reports-read-the-whole-file
A report reads the whole ledger into memory before it sums anything.
Why: The owner's ledger is a few thousand lines; streaming it was turned down as more code for no time saved (measured
on 2026-03-01: 4 ms for 5,000 lines).

blob
mark :23
data 602
### principle no-silent-skips
A line of the ledger that cannot be read fails the command that reads it, saying the line's number and why; nothing is
skipped and nothing is guessed.
Why: A report made over a ledger with a line skipped is wrong without showing it, and the owner trusts the report
(Article 2).

### principle one-file-format
The ledger has one format, `date,amount,category,note` with its header, and every command reads and writes that one.
Why: Two formats drift apart and every command would need both (Article 1); the old semicolon format was converted
once, in 0.3, for that reason.

